424 C programs, inherited, untriaged, and not a pass rate
========================================================

These came over whole from Compiler-C, the C90 compiler this one was forked
from, on 2026-08-26. They are **C**, and cxx1 is a C++11 compiler. Nothing in
the build gates on them: `make test` does not look here, and a case that stops
compiling is as likely to be C++ doing what C++ does as it is to be a fault.

Each program says what it should exit with, in a `// expect: N` line on the
first line. That convention is theirs, not `tests/cases/`'s, which uses sidecar
files instead - and it is the reason these two directories cannot be run by one
script.

Running them
------------

    tests/corpus.sh              all of them, and the split
    tests/corpus.sh add          one, with what it said

Each case is compiled as a `.cpp` copy in `tests/out-corpus/`, with
`pp_helper.h` copied beside it. Since 2026-09-15 (ffc3b77) the driver turns a
`.c` away by name - "looks like C (.c), and cxx1 compiles C++, not C" - and
until this runner copied them every case was refused for that and nothing else:
424 refused, which measured the file extension. The question this corpus asks
is what happens to C when it is read as C++, and a copy asks it without
teaching the driver a `-x` it has never needed.

It exits 0 whatever happens. What it is for is `tests/out-corpus/FAILING`: run
it before a change and after one, and **diff the failing set**. A case that
moves between the lists is worth reading; the count on its own is not.

What the split is on 2026-09-30, at 314eda4, on the Mac
-------------------------------------------------------

    365 ran and agreed, 58 refused, 1 wrong, of 424

The same numbers as 2026-09-02, and the same failing set line for line: the
compiler from just before 314eda4 gives an identical `FAILING`. That commit
removed the implicit `void *` to object-pointer conversion ([conv.ptr]/2),
which C relies on for `p = malloc(n)`, and one case writes it:
`assign_types.c`, `char *w = malloc(4);` on line 21. It was already refused,
four lines earlier, for `char *p = "literal";` - so the first error is
unchanged and nothing moved. Take that line out and the `malloc` one is the
error now, where before 314eda4 the case would have run: a case can hide a
second refusal behind its first, and the failing set shows only the first.
The others that call `malloc` cast its result or keep it in a `void *`. The
59, by cause:

* **45 refused: a string literal where a `char *` is wanted.** 39 are a call -
  `printf(char *, ...)` in 37 of them, `puts` and a `count(char *)` in one each
  - and 6 an initialisation or assignment. In C++11 the literal is `const
  char[N]` and does not convert. cxx1 is right.
* **13 refused at the link: a libc function the case declares itself, without
  `extern "C"`.** `putchar(int)` in 10, `printf(const char *, ...)` in 2 -
  typed correctly, so it compiles, and then its name is mangled - and
  `malloc(long)` in 1. C++ behaving as C++.
* **1 wrong: `pp_predefined`**, as below.

The 2026-09-02 tally below comes to 39 + 7 + 6 = 52 refusals of the 58 it
reports, so six were never accounted for; the likeliest are six more link
failures it does not list - `printf(const char *, ...)` and `malloc(long)`
beside `putchar` - but the failing set of that day was not kept, and that is
a guess. The breakdown above adds up: 45 + 13 = 58.

What the split was on 2026-09-02, on the Mac
--------------------------------------------

    365 ran and agreed, 58 refused, 1 wrong, of 424

Every one of the 59 was read, and none of them is a compiler fault:

* **39 refused: a string literal passed to a `char *` parameter.** The case
  writes `int printf(char *fmt, ...);` and calls it with `"%u\n"`. In C++11 a
  string literal is `const char[N]` and does not convert, so there is no
  matching function. C says yes, C++ says no, and cxx1 is right.
* **7 refused at the link: `putchar(int)` undefined.** The case declares the
  libc function itself, without `extern "C"`, so the name is mangled and the C
  library does not have it. Again C++ behaving as C++; `#include <stdio.h>`
  from `lib/`, which wraps its declarations, links fine.
* **6 refused: the same string-literal rule** reached by an assignment rather
  than a call.
* **1 wrong: `pp_predefined`, expecting 0 and getting 3**, because it asks
  `#ifndef __linux__` and this is a Mac. The corpus was written for one
  machine, so even the wrong bucket has to be read rather than counted.

So the number is not 379/424, which is what this file used to be quoted at in
CLAUDE.md with nothing left in the tree able to reproduce it. The runners that
produced it were deleted with the fork.

Asking clang about one of them
------------------------------

    clang++ -x c++ -std=c++11 -pedantic-errors tests/c-corpus/add.c

`-x c++` is not optional: without it clang compiles the file as C and agrees
with everything. Without `-pedantic-errors` the string-literal rule is a
warning it keeps for old C++, which is the other half of the same trap.
